    {
      "text": "**The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle** is a mystery novel by Stuart Turton, published in 2018 by Sourcebooks Landmark. The book has gained acclaim for its unique narrative structure, where the protagonist relives the same day eight times, each time taking on the identity of a different guest at the Blackheath estate, in a quest to solve the murder of Evelyn Hardcastle. This blending of a classic country house mystery with a time-loop structure has positioned it distinctly within the mystery genre.\n\n### Key Information:\n- **Publication Date:** 2018\n- **Publisher:** Sourcebooks Landmark\n- **ISBN:** 978-1-4926-6854-6\n\n### Major Themes:\n- Recursive narrative\n- Perspective and identity\n- Moral responsibility\n- Country house mystery\n- Time loop\n\n### Awards and Recognition:\nThe novel has been widely reviewed and discussed within literary circles, achieving accolades for its inventive storytelling. It has received several nominations, including the following:\n- **Costa Book Awards** (2018)\n- **The Specsavers National Book Award** for International Author of the Year\n\n### General Positioning in the Genre:\n\"The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle\" has been praised for revitalizing the mystery genre with its innovative structure and has been compared to the works of Agatha Christie, which enhances its appeal to fans of classic mysteries while drawing in new readers with its modern twist. The critical reception highlights its contribution to the evolution of narrative techniques in contemporary fiction.\n\nFor further reading, you might explore these sources:\n- [Wikipedia entry on The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Seven_Deaths_of_Evelyn_Hardcastle)\n- [Critical overview of the novel](https://example.com/reviews/seven-deaths-of-evelyn-hardcastle)\n- [Publisher page for the book](https://example.com/books/seven-deaths-of-evelyn-hardcastle)\n\nThis should give you a solid background for your seminar module on experimental narrative structures!",
