{
  "payloads": [
    {
      "text": "**The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle** revolves around a complex narrative centered on a murder mystery set at the Blackheath estate. The protagonist, who is never explicitly named, finds himself trapped in a time loop, reliving the same day—the day of Evelyn Hardcastle's murder—multiple times. However, each time he experiences the day, he inhabits the body of a different guest at the estate.\n\n### Central Narrative Conceit\n- **Time Loop**: The protagonist wakes up each day in the body of a different character, giving him unique perspectives and insights into the events unfolding around him. This mechanism allows for a layered exploration of each character's motivations, relationships, and secrets.\n- **Body Switching**: Each switch comes with its own set of challenges; the protagonist must not only navigate the relationships and personalities of the hosts he inhabits but also gather clues to solve the murder. He retains his core consciousness but is bound by the limitations and traits of the character he embodies at that moment.\n\nAs he moves through the day, he must uncover who killed Evelyn Hardcastle before the day resets again, forcing him to piece together the fragmented narrative while racing against time—a race that becomes increasingly complex with each new perspective he adopts.\n\nThis engaging structure enables Turton to delve into themes of identity, morality, and the reliability of perspective in storytelling, making it a fascinating read and a rich topic for discussion in your seminar.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
  ],
