    {
      "text": "The critical reception of **The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle** has been generally positive, emphasizing its innovative narrative structure and thematic depth. Here are some notable sources you may consider including in your reading list:\n\n1. **Wikipedia Entry on The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle**  \n   This source offers an overview of the novel's plot and critical reception. It contextualizes how it fits into the mystery genre and discusses its structural elements.  \n   [Read more here](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Seven_Deaths_of_Evelyn_Hardcastle).\n\n2. **A Critical Overview of The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle**  \n   This article reviews the book, highlighting its recursive narrative and exploration of perspectives. It discusses the book's reception among critics and its significance in contemporary fiction.  \n   [Access the overview here](https://openbooks.net/reviews/seven-deaths-of-evelyn-hardcastle).\n\n3. **Stuart Turton: The Seven Deaths of Evelyn Hardcastle — Publisher Page**  \n   The publisher's page provides insights into the book’s description and may include additional resources or links related to critical discussions.  \n   [Visit the publisher page here](https://openbooks.net/books/seven-deaths-of-evelyn-hardcastle).\n\n### Additional Suggestions\nFor scholarly articles discussing the Groundhog Day structure within the murder mystery context, you might want to search databases like JSTOR, Google Scholar, or your institution's library for articles that analyze narrative techniques in contemporary fiction, particularly those that focus on time loops and their impact on storytelling in the mystery genre.\n\nYou may also find it helpful to look for reviews in major publications such as *The New York Times*, *The Guardian*, or *Kirkus Reviews*, as they often provide insights into narrative structures and genre positioning.\n\nThese resources should provide a solid foundation for discussing Turton's narrative styl